Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
you've criticized the government this  morning for  what you call complacency complacency  now you you personally abstained on the  deployment uh of troops to afghanistan a  decade ago just let be clear before we  get into the details before this summer  did you think it was right that british  troops should have been in afghanistan  well  i was skeptical about the reasons for  going into afghanistan 20 years ago but  i very much understood like many people  that there was a real and present threat  from al qaeda that had seen more british  citizens that lose their lives than any  other apart from the united states in  the 9 11 attacks and there was an urgent  and pressing priority to make sure that  the capability of al-qaeda was degraded  and that also therefore meant taking on  the taliban government that was in  charge i think what became apparent very  quickly though was that there was a  problem  with what that led to the secondary  objective which became as joe biden has  said in recent weeks about nation  building and trying to build a  democratic inclusive government in  afghanistan alongside the afghan people  proved to be something that was far more  complicated than those who took us into  afghanistan in the first place envisaged  i think there have been a series of  mistakes over the last 20 years by  political parties of all persuasions on  both sides of the atlantic i think at  some stage there has to be a lessons  learned moment but what was avoidable  and what should have been avoidable was  the chaos that we've seen in recent  weeks that's left us reliant on the  taliban for permission to evacuate our  own citizens and the afghans who helped  us and put many british troops in danger  we had 18 months to plan we shouldn't  have ended up here i'll come to that and  i'll come to that in a second but if i  understand what you said clearly i just  want to be sure that i'm interpreting it  properly  you came around to the view that we  should have been there  but you thought what we did or how we  executed the mission  was poor  no actually i i think that the work that  the british troops did in afghanistan  working alongside the afghan people is  seriously underrated when i talk to  friends in afghanistan the younger  generation who grew up especially girls  being able to access education millions  of them who would never have had those  opportunities there is a whole  generation of young afghans who have  completely different expectations about  their lives than the generation that  went before because of the work that our  british troops did over two decades not  just in terms of education but landmine  clearance which meant that the economic  opportunities for many afghans were  completely transformed there are there  is a lot to be proud of of the work that  our british troops did over 20 years but  one of the things that many are now  asking is was it all in vain because of  the chaos of the last 18 months and they  are questions that we're going to be  putting to the prime minister in the  house of commons tomorrow and that he  simply has to be able to answer  okay well i mean the question for you is  that do you think then that the  president of the united states mr biden  should have stuck to his august the 31st  deadline  or do you think that good work of which  you've just spoken should have been  continued irrespective  i think the problem was the hard  deadline whether it was august 31st or a  different date the problem was setting a  hard deadline for withdrawal and that's  the point that my colleague emily  thornberry who was then the shadow  foreign secretary made at the time that  the doha agreement was struck  with president trump was that a phased  withdrawal  would have meant that the afghan troops  the afghan security forces we could have  continued to work with them in order to  support them to continue that battle  against the taliban but instead a hard  deadline signaled to the taliban that  this was simply a waiting game they knew  that they had until the 31st of august  and then all troops so to be clear and  that appears to have had a major impact  on the morale of the afghan forces as  well so we've been very clear  president biden for that decision  to be clear a labor government  would still have british troops in  afghanistan today  no  no we've never we've never argued that  once the united states took a decision  to withdraw from afghanistan we have  accepted the government position that it  would have been unsustainable to allow  british troops to continue the foreign  secretary was asked this week at the  foreign affairs select committee where  their efforts made with nato partners to  see if an alternative coalition could  have been put together to allow  british troops and others to remain in  afghanistan and he  gave an answer which is very consistent  with what many of those nato countries  have told me and the labour leader  privately  which is that it would have been  completely unsustainable to do that so  the question became how do you have a  chaotic and orderly withdrawal of troops  and how do you continue to support the  afghan people and protect those gains  that we fought for for 20 years after  we've left that's what the government  ought to have been planning for they had  18 months and they simply didn't do it  okay  all right now talking about supporting  the afghan people we are where we are  should our government be talking to the  taliban  or should it be supporting uh  ahmed masood and the national resistance  army in the panchayat valley can't do  both  i think we have to talk to the taliban  and we've been urging the government for  some time to have a dialogue with the  taliban we have thousands of people  still in afghanistan the afghans who  assisted us but british nationals as  well some of those cases are very very  complex the defense secretary has been  suggesting to mps that they should if  they can they should just try to get to  the border any border and see if they  can get across but without safe passage  many of those people will simply not  reach safety we've got british nationals  in wheelchairs we've got children who've  been separated from their parents we've  got somebody who's on dialysis the truth  is that unless if we want to uphold the  promise and commitment that we made to  our own citizens and to many afghans who  assisted us over recent decades we've  got to have a dialogue with the taliban  we've got to ensure as a priority that  safe passage across the country we've  got to be talking to the taliban as well  about the functioning airport and  whether a country like turkey or qatar  could somehow guarantee the safe passage  of our citizens out of that airport this  is really pressing and urgent and there  is no choice at this current stage but  to have a dialogue with the taliban  that's different from diplomatic  recognition um which which is it would  be far too premature to to acknowledge  but dialogue is absolutely essential and  the government is right to have started  that belatedly  so this is  uh a clear statement that you would um  engage with taliban of course uh nobody  in the pancho valley is going to like  that very much are they that will be  seen potentially as a betrayal of those  who have stood out against the taliban  that within days uh  you're talking to people that you  have in recent days actually criticized  for their human rights record  but there's a big difference trevor  between dialogue and diplomatic  recognition this is a regime that came  to power through violence displaced the  democratically elected government that  has been going door-to-door hunting the  uk and people who supported the uk and  the u.s has been rounding up women in  the last 24 hours  and has an appalling human rights record  we've got to be clear-eyed about that  engagement with the taliban and i'm not  for one moment suggesting that we grant  them the legitimacy of diplomatic  recognition as the government of  afghanistan but we have to accept that  because of the chaotic way in which the  government has failed to plan over the  last 18 months we are now in a position  where if we want to evacuate our own  citizens and afghan nationals we've got  to talk to the taliban if we want to  uphold any of the gains of the last 20  years we've got to talk to the taliban  in fact there has been a recognition  even from the outset of the war in  afghanistan that a new government would  mean having some kind of dialogue with  the taliban the united states has been  doing it the united nations has been  doing it and we shouldn't change that  approach now okay um  if i may ask you uh about some domestic  policies this morning the general  secretary of the trade union congress  has called for  essentially a wealth tax  to pay for  uh nhs recovery uh and partic and also  for social care is labor is the labor  leadership going to back that plan  well the first i saw about them was on  the front page of the guardian last  night and i think the broad principle  that francis o'grady is laying out that  those with the broader shoulders should  take some of the burden is absolutely  right fixing the social care crisis is  going to cost a great deal of money and  the prime minister's plan  as we understand it he hasn't shared any  of the details with it but as we  understand it his plan is that he's  going to break his 2019 promise to not  raise national insurance contributions  and load the entirety of the cost of  social care onto the supermarket drivers  supermarket workers the delivery drivers  who are already struggling with high  child care costs high housing costs and  who kept us going through the pandemic i  think that's a really difficult ask of a  group of people who haven't done well  under this conservative government under  the la over the last 11 years and so i  think it's right to say that we need to  think much more creatively about this  and make sure that we don't  load an unsustainable burden onto people  who can't bear it  so so this  so this is labor uh squeezing the rich  until the pips squeak again which in  this context the rich includes people  who live in london and the southeast for  example who have quite a lot of help  wealth but it's wrapped up in houses  which are very expensive  this isn't just going to be billionaires  who pay this  this wealth tax is it  i just i don't really accept any of the  premise of that statement i think that  most people in this country every family  including my own is touched by the  social care crisis it is breaking  families up and down this country and  collectively we've got to find a way to  deal with it if that means that those  who make their money out of  something other than income out of  assets pay a bit more if it means that  people pay a bit more on their income if  it means that we find some kind of  compromise that is sustainable so that  people who can pay a bit more do in  various different ways i think that's  the right approach to take i think we  need to be pragmatic but pensioners on  low incomes who happen to have paid off  their mortgages can't afford can't  generally speaking pay a bit more  well that is the reason why we've got to  learn from the failure to solve this  over the last 20 years because it is  complicated and there will be impacts on  people that  aren't as straightforward as saying one  group of people have to pay for all of  this or another group of people have to  pay for all of this the dill knot report  was an attempt to try to be creative and  a bit more flexible about how we manage  this but we've had this for so long now  in 2009 andy burnham put forward  proposals that collapsed because he  couldn't take the tories with him in  2017 theresa may sprung proposals on on  in her election manifesto that collapsed  because she couldn't take us or other  parties with her and now boris johnson  appears to have sprung a bunch of  proposals on his own party and can't  even take his own party with him we've  got to learn the lessons we've got to  work together to find a fair sustainable  solution to this that commands  thank you and if the prime minister  wants to talk about that our door is  open

